{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>From there they journeyed, etc.</b> One must note why in this journey and the one after it the verse chose to say in this manner <b>from there</b>, etc., and did not say in the manner it said in the preceding passages and the ones before those: ״וַיִּסְעוּ״, etc., ״וַיִּסְעוּ״, etc. Perhaps it is because these two journeys — that of Nahal Zered and that of Nahal Arnon — they journeyed of their own accord and not by the direction of the clouds [of glory] in the usual order, and therefore the verse changed and said <b>from there they journeyed</b>, meaning of their own accord. And I saw fit to prove this interpretation of ours from what the verse states in the portion of Devarim (Deuteronomy 2:13): ״עַתָּה קוּמוּ וְעִבְרוּ לָכֶם אֶת נַחַל זָרֶד״, and it is written (ibid.): ״קוּמוּ סְעוּ וְעִבְרוּ אֶת נַחַל אַרְנוֹן״ — the matter itself indicates that they did not journey by the direction of the cloud, for if it had been by the direction of the cloud, what place would there be for Moshe's words? For when the cloud journeys they journey, and there would have been no need to tell them ״קוּמוּ וְעִבְרוּ״. And although the verse states (Numbers 9:20): ״עַל פִּי ה׳ יַחֲנוּ וְעַל פִּי ה׳ יִסָּעוּ״, this was so for all the journeys except these. And the reason is perhaps that these two journeys were after Aharon died and the clouds of glory were removed, and although they said, of blessed memory (Ta'anit 9a), that they returned in the merit of Moshe, perhaps the cloud by which they would journey and encamp remained withdrawn during the time they journeyed these two mentioned journeys."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:50.697991965Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}